n  electrical engineering at oregon state  jensen's lab partner was a woman named  laurie and these two would eventually  get married  after graduating in 1984 jensen would  score a job at a young startup called  lsi logic the company was founded just  three years before his graduation so  jensen was able to secure a high-level  position as director straight out of  college he didn't stick around for long  though as he would jump to amd and to  become a microprocessor designer at this  point an outsider would think that  jensen was killing it and objectively he  was he had a solid job at amd and he was  presumably building his relationship  with laurie jensen was clearly way ahead  of the average broke college graduate  but jensen wasn't actually that happy  jensen was having some sort of early  life crisis in his late 20s and he  longed to do something that was much  bigger he suppressed this feeling  throughout most of his 20s but  eventually he gave in and took the  plunge  in 1990 jensen returned to school to get  his masters in electrical engineering  from stanford and after graduation he  co-founded a startup with curtis prime  and chris malachovsky  the trio had no clue what to name the  company or how exactly they were going  to fund development but one thing they  were sure of from the very beginning was  that they were going to produce gpus or  graphics processing units  they felt that video games created some  of the world's most computationally  challenging problems and if they could  sell video games they could solve nearly  any computing problem out there aside  from offering a great challenge video  games offered a massive opportunity  gaming was just in its infancy and the  original playstation hadn't even come  out yet so there was plenty of growth  left but the problem was that these  three weren't the only ones trying to  capitalize on this upcoming boom by 1994  there were already over two dozen  graphics card companies and this number  would balloon to over 70 by 1997.  despite the significant competition one  of the best and worst parts of the  semiconductor industry is that brand  value doesn't mean all that much unlike  apple and microsoft who can afford to be  complacent for years and sometimes even  decades being complacent in the  semiconductor industry results in a  pretty quick demotion from number one  regardless of how established you are i  mean just take a look at intel they're  getting obliterated by amd and they  haven't set an all-time high in 21 years  on the bright side this meant that  jensen could easily break into the  market if he created a superior product  the problem though is that it's not  actually that easy to create said  product especially when you only have  forty thousand dollars despite the  difficulty and low budget the trio got  to work on iterating through gpus which  they called nv  nv simply stood for next version and it  wasn't until they incorporated the  company that they came up with a name  for the company itself given that they  were naming all of their prototypes nv  they felt that it was only fitting to  find a name that started with envy for  the company they never found such a word  but they did find a latin word nvidia  which translates to envy they simply  dropped the eye and that's how any video  was born  anyway about a year after finding the  company and many years of working on  their product the trio was ready to  launch their first product in 1994  nvidia formed a partnership with autry  manufacturer sgs thompson and the  diamond multimedia systems to produce  the nv1 sgs was tasked with  manufacturing the chip itself and the  diamond multimedia was tasked with  installing the chips onto multimedia  accelerator boards  the nv1 would launch in 1995 as the  diamond edge 3d and it didn't take long  for video game companies to leverage the  hardware  sega became the first company to port a  3d game called virtual fighter onto the  new gpu over the next few years nvidia  would form a couple of partnerships and  launch a few bits of software and  hardware  but their biggest release by far was in  1999.  in 1999 nvidia launched the geforce 256  which they like to call the world's  first gpu  this doesn't really mean much though  given that they invented the term  themselves  nvidia defined gpu as a single chip  processor that could process a minimum  of 10 million polygons per second to put  that in perspective modern gpus are able  to process 7 billion polygons per second  so there was still a long way to go but  this is where it all started  before nvidia could focus on reaching  those insane numbers though they needed  to focus on survival because the dot-com  crash was rolling around nvidia was  founded right before the dock on bubble  really started to pick up steam so their  rise up was like a rocket ship but now  it was time to see if nvidia could reach  orbit or if they were going to crash  back to earth the dot-com crash didn't  initially impact nvidia as they were  more of a background player as opposed  to a consumer brand  in fact nvidia was named the  fastest-growing company in america in  2002  ironically though nvidia would get  crushed that same year within just 12  months nvidia would crash 90 percent and  nearly return to ipo levels  to make things worse curtis prime would  retire from nvidia the following year  so it was up to jensen and chris to lead  the company through the wreckage  jensen started off by acquiring some  discounted companies including 3dfx and  media q but nvidia's biggest development  during this time was no doubt sli  nowadays sli is mostly a joke and there  is very little use for it but back in  2004 this was a game changer as a lie or  scalable link interface allowed multiple  gpus to work together and produce a  singular output  if you had two gpus and sli each gpu  would render alternating frames so the  workload of each gpu was cut in half now  this didn't mean that the system was  twice as powerful as a single gpu but it  was a substantial improvement  also this invention resulted in gaming  enthusiasts to buy multiple gpus each  time they built a pc so it was quite  lucrative aside from sli nvidia also  formed a handful of strategic  partnerships  in 2004 nvidia formed a partnership with  nasa where they helped nasa reconstruct  the train of mars using nvidia gpus that  same year nvidia would also team up with  blizzard entertainment to create 3d  graphics for world of warcraft  nvidia followed up these great  partnerships with the nail in the coffin  which was a partnership with sony on the  ps3 in 2005. with all of these  acquisitions partnerships and sli nvidia  was able to survive the dot-com crash  remember how there were 70 companies in  1997 targeting the gpu market well by  2006 nvidia was the only independent gpu  company that was still standing it  seemed like it was finally just blue  skies ahead and forbes would even name  nvidia as the company of the year in  2007  and then the 2008 financial crisis  rolled around after a fortune named  nvidia the fastest growing company of  the year in 2002 nvidia crashed 90  percent  after forbes named nvidia the company of  the year in 2007 2008 happened  fortunately 2008 wasn't nearly as bad as  a dotcom crash for nvidia  in terms of stock price nvidia still  crashed 85 percent but fundamentally the  company was much stronger the company  did have a couple of negative quarters  but they were back up on their feet by  2010 and that brings us into modern day  nvidia  throughout the first half of the 2010s  nvidia was more or less stagnant their  revenue and profits didn't change all  that much and the company wasn't really  growing this wasn't really their fault  though as they had simply reached market  saturation they needed the market itself  to grow larger before they could grow  again  fortunately for nvidia the market would  not only grow but it would explode in  2016 and 17 due to cryptocurrencies  as you all probably know bitcoin made a  mad dash at 20 000 and they took all the  all coins with it to the moon of 2017.  graphics cards happen to be perfect for  cryptocurrency mining and especially  ethereum mining  miners ate up the gpu market as they  were willing to pay anything to get  their hands on gpus  double triple quadruple the price miners  didn't care  they were making thousands if not tens  of thousands from their mining rigs and  paying a premium to get a gpu was not a  problem nvidia saw a supply shortage  like never before and they were able to  jack up prices through the roof between  2016 and 2018 nvidia's quarterly profits  jumped from 200 million a quarter to 1.2  billion dollars per quarter and this was  just the beginning given that nvidia had  reached market saturation with gamers  jensen was shifting his focus to other  markets which led him to deep learning  and ai they formed a partnership with  the tesla and provided them with the  initial hardware for self-driving they  also launched a bunch of commercial  cards like the titan and quattro that  appealed to institutional buyers just as  bitcoin was blowing up and their  expansion efforts were paying off amd  was resurrecting the stagnant pc gaming  community the launch of ryzen cpus made  pcs much more affordable which attracted  a lot of console gamers by the end of  2018 and throughout 2019 though the  effects of these catalysts were starting  to wear off but then 2020 and the supply  shortage hit and nvidia was sent up on  another rocket ship and they haven't  gotten off yet  today jensen stands at 27.9 billion  dollars which makes him part of the top  100 richest people in the world  looking forward it's not clear how much  more nvidia can grow in the immediate  future as they've already bloomed 129  over the past year  but as long as jensen is ceo i think  it's just a matter of time until nvidia  recovers from the next setback and  becomes a trillion dollar company and  then nvidia will be the trillion dollar  shadow company do you guys own an nvidia  gpu comment that down below  also drop a like if you're still waiting  for gpu prices to go down  and of course consider joining our  discord community to suggest future  video ideas and consider subscribing to  see more questions logically answered  but until then i'm hari and i'll see you  guys on the next one

